 "transcript": {
  "answer": "It's not just you. Your bill has probably gone up a lot, but mostly because government rebates ended, not because Perth power got much more expensive.\n\n**The actual price has gone up slowly.** The ABS electricity price index for Perth goes up about 2.5% every July: 92.9 (2023) → 95.2 → 97.6 → 100.0 → 102.4 (July 2026). In the latest data, **August 2026**, Perth electricity was **2.4% higher than a year earlier**. That's below Perth's overall inflation of 4.3%.\n\n**Rebates made the last couple of years look cheap.** The ABS counts state and federal bill credits as price cuts, and they made Perth's index swing a lot:\n- In August 2024 to February 2025 the index fell to **25.7**, roughly a quarter of the full price, because the credits wiped out most of the bill.\n- In October 2025 to January 2026 there was another dip, to **69–90**.\n- Since **February 2026** the index has stayed at the full price (**100**, then **102.4** after July). There have been no credits in the data since then.\n\nSo the jump you're feeling is mostly the credits running out. You're paying close to the full cost for the first time in a while. That's also why the yearly change briefly showed around **+289%** in February 2026: it was compared with February 2025, when the bill was mostly rebated.\n\n**Perth is doing better than the rest of the country:**\n\n| Electricity prices | Perth | Australia |\n|---|---|---|\n| Change in the year to August 2026 | **+2.4%** | **+13.2%** |\n| Jan 2023 → Aug 2026 | ~+10% | ~+18% |\n\nEast-coast prices have gone up much faster than Perth's regulated tariff.\n\n**Other possible reasons:** This data tracks prices only, not how much power you use. A hot summer or a cold winter, more people at home or a new appliance could make your bill bigger even at the same price. Comparing your kWh usage with the same bill last year will show you which it is.\n\nIn short, it's fair to feel the bill is huge compared with recent years, but the price per unit has gone up only about 2.4% in the past year.",
